// Firmware Engineer. Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D', 'E'|'M'|'S'|'X', source, { t: top, f: [follow-up q, a] }]
QB["hw-fw"] = (QB["hw-fw"] || []).concat([
["Role overview", "What does a firmware engineer do?", "**Why they ask:** the opening question.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I write the software that runs directly on microcontrollers and processors inside devices. That includes device drivers for peripherals and sensors, real-time control logic, communication protocols, power management, bootloaders and firmware updates. I work closely with hardware engineers during bring-up, and I spend a lot of time debugging with a JTAG debugger, logic analyzer and scope. Mostly C, some C++, and Python for test tools.\"\n\nTip: Mention bring-up and debugging tools — firmware lives close to hardware.", "B", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["Which microcontrollers have you used?","Mostly ARM Cortex-M parts from ST and Nordic, plus some ESP32."]}],
["C fundamentals", "What does the volatile keyword do, and when do you need it?", "**Why they ask:** a classic firmware screening question.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Volatile tells the compiler the variable can change outside the normal program flow, so it must read it from memory every time instead of keeping it in a register. You need it for hardware registers, variables shared with interrupt handlers, and memory changed by DMA or another core. It doesn't make access atomic or thread-safe — that needs other protection.\"\n\nTip: Add that volatile is not atomic — it shows depth.", "B", "E", null, {"t":1,"f":["Give an example of a bug from a missing volatile.","A flag set in an interrupt but read in a loop — without volatile the compiler can read it once and loop forever."]}],
["Interrupts", "What are the rules for writing a good interrupt handler?", "**Why they ask:** interrupts cause many hard bugs.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Keep it short — capture data, clear the flag, set a flag or push to a queue, and do the heavy work in the main loop or a task. No blocking calls, no printf, avoid floating point unless the core saves context cheaply. Protect shared data — disable interrupts briefly or use atomic operations. And set priorities thoughtfully so critical interrupts aren't delayed.\"\n\nTip: 'Do the minimum, defer the rest' is the core rule.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["What is a race condition between an ISR and main code?","When both read and modify shared data and an interrupt lands in the middle, leaving the data inconsistent."]}],
["RTOS", "When would you use an RTOS instead of a super loop?", "**Why they ask:** architecture judgement.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A simple super loop with interrupts is fine for small, simple devices. An RTOS like FreeRTOS or Zephyr helps when there are several independent activities with different timing needs — like a sensor, a radio stack and a user interface — because tasks with priorities make timing easier to reason about. The cost is memory, complexity and new bug types like priority inversion and stack overflow. I pick the simplest option that meets the timing requirements.\"\n\nTip: Show you know the costs, not just the benefits.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["What is priority inversion?","A high-priority task waits for a resource held by a low-priority task that is being pre-empted by a medium task — priority inheritance on mutexes fixes it."]}],
["Memory", "How do you manage memory on a small microcontroller?", "**Why they ask:** RAM and flash are tight.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I avoid dynamic allocation after startup — malloc can fragment and fail at runtime — and use static buffers or memory pools. I size task stacks by measuring high-water marks, keep constant data in flash, and check the linker map to see what uses space. I add stack overflow detection during development. Memory use is reviewed as part of every release.\"\n\nTip: 'No malloc after init' is the expected answer.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["How do you find a stack overflow?","Fill the stack with a known pattern and check how much is overwritten, or use the RTOS's overflow hooks and the MPU."]}],
["Drivers", "How do you write a driver for a new sensor?", "**Why they ask:** day-to-day firmware work.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I read the datasheet for the interface, timing, register map and startup sequence. I write a thin layer for the bus — I2C or SPI — and a driver on top with init, read and configure functions and clear error codes. I check the chip ID at startup, handle timeouts and bus errors, and write unit tests with a mocked bus. Then I verify on hardware with a logic analyzer to confirm the traffic matches the datasheet.\"\n\nTip: Error handling and a mocked bus show quality habits.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["Why separate the bus layer from the driver?","So the driver can be reused on other boards and tested without hardware."]}],
["Updates", "How do you design safe over-the-air firmware updates?", "**Why they ask:** failed updates can brick devices.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Use two image slots — the device downloads the new image into the inactive slot while the current one keeps running. The bootloader checks the image's signature and integrity before switching. After switching, the new firmware must confirm it's healthy, otherwise the bootloader rolls back. Power loss at any point must leave a bootable image. Signing prevents anyone from loading unauthorized firmware.\"\n\nTip: Dual slots, signature check and rollback are the three pillars.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why sign firmware images?","So the device only runs firmware from you, which stops attackers from installing modified code."]}],
["Debugging", "How do you debug a hard fault on an ARM Cortex-M?", "**Why they ask:** a very common, tricky problem.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I capture the fault registers and the stacked program counter and link register in the fault handler, which show the instruction that faulted and the caller. The fault status registers tell me the type — like a bad memory access or a divide by zero. Common causes are null or wild pointers, stack overflow, unaligned access, or calling a function through a corrupted pointer. Then I reproduce it under the debugger with a breakpoint near that address.\"\n\nTip: Mention reading the stacked PC — that's the key skill.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What would you log from a field device that faults?","The fault registers, PC, LR and a short trace into a reserved memory area, sent home after reboot."]}],
["Low power", "How do you minimize power consumption in firmware?", "**Why they ask:** battery devices depend on firmware.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Spend as much time as possible in the deepest sleep mode that still meets wake-up needs. Use interrupts and timers to wake instead of polling, turn off peripherals and clocks when idle, batch work so the device wakes less often, and use DMA so the CPU can sleep during transfers. Then I measure current with a power profiler, because one forgotten peripheral can double consumption.\"\n\nTip: Measure, don't assume.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["Why is polling bad for power?","The CPU stays awake checking for events instead of sleeping until an interrupt wakes it."]}],
["Testing", "How do you test firmware?", "**Why they ask:** quality practices.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Unit tests on the host for logic, with hardware access mocked — using a framework like Unity or GoogleTest. Integration tests on real hardware, ideally automated in a rack with power control and a debugger. Static analysis and compiler warnings set to errors. And for safety-related products, coding standards like MISRA C. Tests run in continuous integration on every change.\"\n\nTip: Host-based unit tests are a modern practice worth naming.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["What is hardware-in-the-loop testing?","Testing firmware on real hardware while a test system simulates inputs and checks outputs automatically."]}],
["Behavioral", "Tell me about a difficult firmware bug you fixed.", "**Why they ask:** debugging approach.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A device locked up about once a week. I added a small trace buffer logging task switches and key events into RAM that survived resets. After a lockup it showed a deadlock: two tasks took the same two mutexes in opposite order. I changed the code to always take them in the same order and added a lock-order check in debug builds. The lockups stopped.\"\n\nTip: Show how you built tools to capture rare bugs.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["How do you prevent deadlocks in general?","Take locks in a consistent order, hold them briefly, and avoid needing two at once where possible."]}],
["Closing", "Why do you want this firmware role?", "**Why they ask:** motivation and fit.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I like working where software meets hardware — seeing code move a motor or read a sensor. Your connected products need reliable, low-power firmware with safe updates, which is exactly what I've built. I'd bring good testing habits and debugging tools, and I want to grow toward firmware architecture.\"\n\nTip: Tie your skills to their product needs.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["Would you work on Linux-based devices too?","Yes — I've done some embedded Linux driver and Yocto work and enjoy both."]}]
]);

