QB["hw-fw"] = (QB["hw-fw"] || []).concat([
["Struct packing", "What problems come from struct padding and alignment in firmware?", "**Why they ask:** a frequent source of subtle bugs.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The compiler inserts padding between struct members so each is naturally aligned, which means a struct's layout in memory isn't just the sum of its fields. That bites when I overlay a struct on a hardware register block or a received packet and the padding doesn't match what the hardware or sender expects, or when I assume a struct is a certain size. I handle it by designing register and protocol structs with members ordered to avoid padding, using the compiler's packed attribute when I truly need no padding — knowing packed access can be slower or fault on unaligned-access cores — and never relying on implicit layout for anything that crosses a boundary.\"\n\nTip: Padding makes struct layout non-obvious; order members well, use packed carefully, and don't overlay unpacked structs on registers or packets.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What's the risk of a packed struct on some cores?","Members may end up unaligned, and a core that can't do unaligned access will fault or the compiler emits slow byte-by-byte access."]}],
["Static keyword", "Explain the different meanings of 'static' in C firmware.", "**Why they ask:** tests real C fluency.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Static has three uses. On a file-scope variable or function it gives internal linkage — it's private to that file, which I use to hide a module's internals and avoid name clashes. On a local variable inside a function it gives static storage duration — the variable persists across calls and keeps its value, and it lives in .data or .bss, not on the stack, which matters for memory and for reentrancy. The common thread is 'static storage, limited visibility.' In firmware I use file-scope static liberally for encapsulation and am careful with function-static variables because they make a function non-reentrant.\"\n\nTip: File-scope static = private to the file; local static = persists across calls (and makes the function non-reentrant).", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why does a function-local static hurt reentrancy?","The persisted variable is shared across all calls, so if an ISR or another task calls the function concurrently they corrupt each other's state."]}],
["Reentrancy", "What makes a function reentrant, and why does it matter?", "**Why they ask:** reentrancy bugs are brutal to debug.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A reentrant function can be safely called again before a prior call finishes — from an ISR interrupting a task, or from multiple tasks — because it doesn't rely on shared mutable state. That means no static or global variables modified without protection, no non-reentrant library calls, and working only on its own arguments and locals. It matters because firmware has interrupts and tasks calling the same code; a non-reentrant function corrupts its own state when re-entered. I make shared helpers reentrant, pass state via parameters, and if a function must touch shared data, I protect it with a critical section or a lock.\"\n\nTip: Reentrant = no unprotected shared state; essential when ISRs and tasks call the same code.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Is the standard library always safe to call from an ISR?","No — functions like malloc or some printf implementations aren't reentrant or are slow; you avoid them in ISRs or use reentrant variants."]}],
["Avoiding malloc", "Why do many firmware teams avoid dynamic memory allocation?", "**Why they ask:** memory discipline is expected in embedded.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"On a small, long-running system with limited RAM, heap allocation brings risks that are hard to tolerate: fragmentation over time can make an allocation fail even when total free memory is enough, allocation timing is non-deterministic which hurts real-time behavior, and a leak in a device that runs for months will eventually crash. So many teams allocate statically or from fixed pools sized at design time, which is deterministic and provably bounded. If dynamic memory is needed, I use fixed-size block pools to avoid fragmentation and bound the time. The goal is predictable, bounded memory use with no slow leak.\"\n\nTip: Static or fixed-pool allocation avoids fragmentation, non-determinism and slow leaks on long-running devices.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["How does a fixed-size block pool avoid fragmentation?","All blocks are the same size, so any freed block fits any future request — there's no way to leave unusable gaps the way variable-size allocation does."]}],
["Clock config", "What do you check when configuring an MCU's clock tree?", "**Why they ask:** clock misconfiguration causes mysterious failures.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I set up the clock source — internal RC or an external crystal — through the PLL to the core and bus clocks, respecting every constraint: the PLL input and output ranges, the maximum frequency of each bus and peripheral, and the flash wait states needed at the target speed, because running flash too fast without wait states corrupts instruction fetches. I enable the clock to each peripheral before using it, and I verify the actual frequency, often by outputting it on a pin, since an unstable crystal or a wrong divider silently makes every timed thing — baud rates, delays, PWM — wrong. Getting the clock right is a prerequisite for everything else.\"\n\nTip: Respect PLL ranges, bus max frequencies and flash wait states; enable peripheral clocks; verify the real frequency.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why do flash wait states matter at high clock speeds?","Flash is slower than the core; without enough wait states the CPU fetches before data is ready, causing corruption — so wait states scale with clock frequency."]}],
["ADC sampling", "What do you watch for when sampling an analog signal with an ADC?", "**Why they ask:** signal-chain firmware is common.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"First, sample fast enough — above twice the highest frequency of interest — and anti-alias filter before the ADC, because any content above half the sample rate folds back as a false low-frequency alias I can't remove in software. I give the sample-and-hold enough acquisition time for the source impedance so the reading settles, use a stable reference since the result is relative to it, and average or oversample to reduce noise. I watch grounding and when the ADC samples relative to noisy switching events, sometimes triggering conversions away from switch-node transitions. Accurate ADC work is as much about the analog setup and timing as the code.\"\n\nTip: Respect Nyquist, anti-alias filter before the ADC, allow acquisition time for the source impedance, and use a stable reference.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why can't you filter out aliasing in software after sampling?","Once a high frequency folds below Nyquist it's indistinguishable from a real low-frequency signal, so it must be filtered in analog before the ADC."]}],
["Debouncing", "How do you debounce a mechanical button in firmware?", "**Why they ask:** a classic entry-level but revealing question.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A mechanical switch bounces for a few milliseconds, so a raw read gives multiple false transitions. I debounce by sampling the pin periodically — say every few milliseconds in a timer — and only accepting a state change after it's been stable for a set number of consecutive samples, or by starting a short timer on the first edge and reading again after the bounce settles. I prefer the periodic-sample approach because it's robust and doesn't block. For many buttons I integrate a counter per button. I avoid debouncing by busy-delay in an ISR, which wastes time and can miss events.\"\n\nTip: Sample periodically and require N stable samples before accepting a change — don't busy-delay in an ISR.", "B", "E", null, {"f":["Can you debounce in hardware instead?","Yes — an RC filter plus a Schmitt-trigger input, or an SR latch for some switches — which offloads it from firmware, though software debouncing is cheaper and more flexible."]}],
["Brown-out", "Why is brown-out detection important and how do you use it?", "**Why they ask:** power-supply edge cases corrupt firmware.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"If the supply sags below the MCU's safe operating voltage but not to zero, the core can execute wrongly, corrupt RAM, or write bad data to flash — worse than a clean reset. A brown-out detector holds the MCU in reset whenever the supply is below a threshold, so it only runs when the voltage is adequate and comes up cleanly when power returns. I enable it set above the minimum operating voltage for the clock speed, and I'm especially careful that flash writes don't happen during marginal voltage, since a half-completed flash write during a brown-out is a classic cause of corrupted configuration or bricked devices.\"\n\nTip: Brown-out keeps the MCU in reset below a safe voltage, preventing corrupt execution and torn flash writes during sags.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why are flash writes during a brown-out especially dangerous?","A write interrupted by falling voltage can leave flash in an indeterminate state, corrupting configuration or the application image."]}],
["Reset cause", "Why check the reset cause on startup?", "**Why they ask:** diagnosing field failures starts here.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The MCU records why it last reset — power-on, external pin, watchdog, brown-out, or software — and reading that at startup tells me a lot. A watchdog reset means firmware hung and I should log it and maybe enter a safe mode; repeated brown-out resets point at a power problem; a clean power-on is normal. I log the cause to non-volatile storage with a counter so field units build a history, which turns 'it randomly reboots' into 'it's watchdog-resetting every few hours,' a far more actionable diagnosis. It's a cheap feature that pays off hugely in debugging deployed devices.\"\n\nTip: Read and log the reset cause at boot — it turns vague 'random reboots' into a specific, actionable failure mode.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What would repeated watchdog resets in the field tell you?","That the firmware is hanging periodically; you'd capture state before the reset and look for a deadlock, infinite loop, or a blocking call that doesn't return."]}],
["MISRA", "What is MISRA C and static analysis, and how do you use them?", "**Why they ask:** quality and safety processes.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"MISRA C is a set of coding rules that restrict the language to a safer subset — avoiding undefined behavior, risky implicit conversions, and error-prone constructs — widely used in automotive, medical and other safety-related firmware. A static analyzer checks code against these rules and also flags likely bugs — uninitialized variables, out-of-bounds, null dereferences, dead code — without running it. I run static analysis in the build or CI so issues surface early, triage and fix real findings, and justify any deliberate rule deviations with documented rationale. It catches whole classes of bugs that testing might miss, especially the ones that only bite in the field.\"\n\nTip: MISRA restricts C to a safer subset; static analysis catches bug classes pre-runtime — run it in CI and justify deviations.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Does passing static analysis mean the code is bug-free?","No — it catches certain classes of defects, but logic errors and timing bugs still need testing; it's one layer, not a guarantee."]}],
["Host testing", "How do you unit-test firmware, and what runs on the host versus the target?", "**Why they ask:** testability separates mature firmware teams.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I structure code so the logic — protocol parsing, state machines, algorithms — is separated from the hardware access behind a thin driver layer. Then I unit-test the logic on the host PC with a normal test framework, mocking or faking the hardware layer, which runs fast and in CI on every commit. Hardware-dependent code and timing I test on the target or on hardware-in-the-loop rigs. This split lets me test most of the complexity quickly off-target and reserve the slower target testing for what truly needs real hardware. The key enabler is decoupling logic from direct register access.\"\n\nTip: Decouple logic from hardware so most of it unit-tests on the host in CI; reserve target/HIL testing for hardware and timing.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["What's hardware-in-the-loop testing?","Running the real firmware on real or simulated hardware while a test rig injects inputs and checks outputs, validating behavior the host can't — timing, peripherals, and real signals."]}],
["CI for firmware", "What does a CI pipeline for firmware look like?", "**Why they ask:** modern firmware uses CI too.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"At minimum CI builds the firmware for every commit so a broken build is caught immediately, runs static analysis and the host-side unit tests, and checks formatting and code-size against a budget so I don't silently blow the flash. More advanced pipelines flash a connected target or a hardware-in-the-loop rig and run integration tests, and produce a versioned, signed artifact with a build ID baked in so any binary is traceable to its source. I tag releases and keep the exact toolchain pinned so builds are reproducible. CI turns firmware from 'works on my machine' into repeatable, tested, traceable builds.\"\n\nTip: Build + static analysis + host tests on every commit, size budget check, and reproducible, versioned, signed artifacts.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why pin the toolchain version in CI?","Compiler versions can change code generation and warnings; pinning makes builds reproducible so a binary can be rebuilt identically later."]}],
["Debugging tools", "How do you debug a timing problem you can't catch in a debugger?", "**Why they ask:** real-time bugs vanish under a breakpoint.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Breakpoints stop time, so they hide race and timing bugs. Instead I make timing visible without disturbing it: toggle a spare GPIO at the start and end of an ISR or task and watch it on a scope or logic analyzer to measure execution time, latency and jitter directly. For data I use a fast, non-blocking trace — a ring buffer in RAM I read later, or an ARM ITM/RTT channel — rather than slow blocking printf. A logic analyzer decodes bus traffic to see exactly what the peripheral did. The theme is observing the running system with minimal perturbation.\"\n\nTip: GPIO toggles on a scope, non-blocking trace (RTT/ITM), and a logic analyzer reveal timing that breakpoints destroy.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why is printf a poor choice for debugging timing?","It's slow and often blocking, so it changes the timing you're trying to measure; a GPIO toggle or RTT is near-zero-overhead."]}],
["CAN", "What's important about CAN bus firmware?", "**Why they ask:** CAN is ubiquitous in automotive and industrial.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"CAN is a robust, multi-master bus where messages carry identifiers, not node addresses, and the identifier sets priority through bitwise arbitration — the lower-ID message wins the bus without collisions. In firmware I configure the bit timing carefully so every node agrees on the sample point, set up acceptance filters so the controller only interrupts me for relevant IDs, and handle the error states: CAN counts errors and moves a node from error-active to error-passive to bus-off, so I monitor that and implement recovery. I respect the protocol's built-in error detection rather than reinventing it, and design message sets and rates to fit the bus load.\"\n\nTip: ID-based priority arbitration, careful bit-timing/sample-point, acceptance filters, and handling the error-state machine up to bus-off.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What does 'bus-off' mean and how do you recover?","A node's transmit error count exceeded the limit, so it removes itself from the bus; recovery requires a defined reset/restart sequence, often after observing the bus is idle."]}],
["Error handling", "What's your philosophy on error handling and asserts in firmware?", "**Why they ask:** robust firmware handles the unexpected deliberately.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Every call that can fail gets its result checked — I don't ignore return codes — and I decide deliberately what each failure means: retry, fall back, or enter a safe state. For programming errors that should never happen, I use asserts during development to catch them loudly, but in production I replace a hard halt with a logged fault and a controlled recovery, because a deployed device shouldn't just freeze. The guiding idea is to fail safe and observably: get the hardware to a safe condition, log enough to diagnose it, and recover if possible rather than hang silently. Unhandled errors are how field failures start.\"\n\nTip: Check every fallible call, use asserts in dev, and in the field log the fault and go to a safe state rather than hang.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why not leave asserts as hard halts in production?","A halt in the field just bricks the device for the user; logging the fault and entering a safe, recoverable state is better unless halting is genuinely the safest action."]}],
["Safe state", "What does 'fail to a safe state' mean for a firmware-controlled device?", "**Why they ask:** safety thinking for devices that act on the world.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"If firmware detects a fault — a sensor out of range, a watchdog recovery, a failed self-check — it should drive the system's outputs to whatever condition causes the least harm, not keep operating on bad data. For a motor that might mean removing drive and engaging a brake; for a heater, turning it off; for a valve, moving to a defined position. I define the safe state per actuator during design, make sure it's reached even if software is partly broken — sometimes with hardware interlocks independent of the MCU — and I don't assume the firmware that detected the fault is healthy enough to do anything complex. Safe-state design is about minimizing harm when things go wrong.\"\n\nTip: On a fault, drive outputs to the least-harmful defined state, backed by hardware interlocks that don't depend on healthy firmware.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why back the safe state with hardware, not just firmware?","Because the firmware that should command the safe state may itself be the thing that failed; an independent hardware interlock still protects the system."]}],
["WCET", "How do you ensure a real-time deadline is always met?", "**Why they ask:** hard real-time is about worst case, not average.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A deadline is met only if the worst-case execution time plus the worst-case blocking and interrupt interference still fits before the deadline — the average doesn't matter, the worst case does. So I analyze the critical path's worst-case timing, account for the longest any higher-priority interrupt or critical section can delay it, and avoid unbounded operations like variable-length loops, dynamic allocation, or blocking calls on that path. I measure actual worst-case timing with GPIO toggles under stress, not just typical runs, and leave margin. For hard deadlines I keep the time-critical work deterministic and bounded by design.\"\n\nTip: Meet deadlines on worst-case time plus interference, not average — keep the critical path bounded and measure under stress.", "D", "X", null, {"f":["Why avoid dynamic allocation on a real-time path?","Its timing is non-deterministic and can vary with fragmentation, so it breaks the bounded worst-case timing a hard deadline needs."]}],
["Double buffering", "What is double buffering and where do you use it in firmware?", "**Why they ask:** a key pattern for streaming data.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Double buffering uses two buffers so one can be filled while the other is processed, avoiding the need to stop the stream. A classic use is DMA from an ADC or into a display: DMA fills buffer A while firmware processes buffer B, then they swap on the half or full transfer interrupt — this gives continuous, gap-free data and decouples the fast hardware stream from the slower processing. I make sure processing of one buffer always finishes before DMA comes back around to overwrite it, which sets a real-time constraint, and I handle the swap atomically so I never read a buffer that's being written.\"\n\nTip: Fill one buffer while processing the other and swap on the DMA half/full interrupt — gives gap-free streaming if processing keeps up.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What breaks if processing is too slow for double buffering?","DMA laps around and overwrites a buffer before it's processed, dropping or corrupting data — so the processing must keep pace with the stream rate."]}],
["Behavioral", "Tell me about a firmware bug that only appeared occasionally. How did you find it?", "**Why they ask:** intermittent bugs test real debugging skill.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"We had a device that locked up roughly once a day. Because it was rare and a debugger would have changed the timing, I added lightweight instrumentation first: logged the reset cause and a small trace ring buffer to non-volatile memory, and toggled GPIOs on key ISRs to watch on a logic analyzer during soak. The reset cause showed it was the watchdog, and the trace showed the main loop stopped servicing after a particular interrupt burst. It turned out to be a race on a shared flag between an ISR and the main loop that only lined up under heavy traffic. I fixed it with a proper critical section and confirmed with a long soak test.\"\n\nTip: A concrete intermittent-bug story — log reset cause, trace buffer, soak test, found a race — shows systematic debugging.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why not just reproduce it under the debugger?","A breakpoint stops time and hides the race; non-intrusive logging and soak testing let the bug happen naturally so you can catch its signature."]}]
]);
